window.SPL_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Built-ins","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html","level":1,"text":"A deliberately small, Python-flavoured standard library - enough to validate, reshape and report on LLM output without leaving the language."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#functions","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Raw tools: no model round-trip","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#raw-tools","level":3,"text":"read_file(path), write_file(path, content), web_fetch(url), web_search(query), and shell(command) are ordinary positional functions with required string arguments. They use active restrictions, return raw text (write returns confirmation), and never invoke a model. Shell returns combined stdout/stderr or raises tool_error on nonzero exit. Raw results are not truncated. See tool contracts. Use a se"},{"title":"Types & conversion","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#conversion","level":3,"text":"SignatureDescription len(x)Length of a string (in characters), list or dict. str(x)Display form: strings as is, True/False/None, numbers plainly, lists and dicts as JSON. int(x)Truncates a number; converts True/False to 1/0; parses an integer string. float(x)Numbers pass through; booleans become 1/0; numeric strings are parsed. bool(x)Truthiness of any value. type(x)One of 'None' 'bool' 'number' '"},{"title":"Numbers & sequences","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#numbers","level":3,"text":"SignatureDescription range(stop)range(start, stop[, step])List of integers; step may be negative but not zero. abs(n)Absolute value. round(n[, digits])Without digits: rounds half to even (round(2.5) is 2). With digits: rounds to that many decimal places. min(x) / max(x)min(a, b, …)Smallest / largest of an iterable or of the arguments. Empty input is an error. sum(x)Sum of an iterable of numbers. s"},{"title":"Dicts & JSON","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#dict-fns","level":3,"text":"SignatureDescription keys(d) values(d) items(d)Lists of keys, values, or [key, value] pairs. json_parse(s)Parses a JSON string into a value. Invalid JSON raises runtime_error. json_stringify(v[, indent])Serializes to JSON, optionally pretty-printed with indent spaces."},{"title":"Logging","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#logging","level":3,"text":"SignatureDescription log(a, b, …)log{level=debug}(a, b, …)Writes the str() of each argument, space-separated, to stderr. Plain calls default to info; the modifier selects error, info or debug, using a bare name, string or expression. The level is evaluated once and validated before arguments. Debug messages appear only at debug log level; silent suppresses all logs. Returns None. Never pollutes st"},{"title":"Methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#methods","level":2,"text":"Methods are called with dot syntax on strings, lists and dicts. Strings are immutable; list and dict methods that say \"in place\" mutate the value and return None."},{"title":"String methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#string-methods","level":3,"text":"MethodDescription upper() lower()Change case. title() capitalize()Title-case every word / capitalize the first character. strip() lstrip() rstrip()Trim whitespace from both / left / right. split([sep])Without a separator: split on runs of whitespace. With one: split on exactly that text (empty separator is an error). splitlines()Split on \\n or \\r\\n. An empty string gives []. join(items)', '.join(p"},{"title":"List methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#list-methods","level":3,"text":"MethodDescription append(x)Add an item at the end (in place). extend(iterable)Append all items of another iterable (in place). insert(i, x)Insert before index i (in place). pop([i])Remove and return the item at i (default: last). remove(x)Remove the first item equal to x; error if missing (in place). index(x)Index of the first equal item, or -1. count(x)Number of equal items. sort([reverse]) rever"},{"title":"Dict methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#dict-methods","level":3,"text":"MethodDescription keys() values() items()Lists of keys, values, [key, value] pairs. get(key[, default])Value for key, or default (None if omitted). The safe alternative to d.key, which fails on a missing key. pop(key[, default])Remove and return a key; error if missing and no default. update(other)Merge another dict in (in place). copy() clear()Shallow copy / remove all keys. 💡 Safe access to mod"},{"title":"Putting it together","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#example","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"CLI & runtime","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html","level":1,"text":"The interpreter runs one script file to completion. It prints the result on stdout, logs on stderr and reports the outcome through its exit code."},{"title":"Usage","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#usage","level":2,"text":"From a checkout use node dist/cli.js or npx file:./lang. The package installs two equivalent binaries: spl and spl-lang. A script run expects exactly one script file; login and logout are separate commands, see Authentication."},{"title":"Options","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#options","level":2,"text":"OptionDefaultDescription --log-level <level>infoerror, info or debug. Logs go to stderr. --silentoffSuppress all logs; only the result is printed. --model <id>SDK defaultOverrides per-call model, then header model, then SDK default. --mcp <file.json>noneMCP servers in VS Code mcp.json format; see MCP configuration. --store <file.json>-Persist top-level variables in a JSON file. --store-module <pat"},{"title":"Output streams & exit codes","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#streams","level":2,"text":"stdout The value of success or of a top-level return: strings raw, everything else as pretty-printed JSON. On an uncaught failure: the failure payload. stderr Logs, log() output, syntax errors, and for every LLM call the effective model and reasoning effort. Exit codeMeaning 0Success. 1Uncaught script failure. 2Syntax or usage error. 70Internal error in the interpreter."},{"title":"Logging","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#logging","level":2,"text":"LevelShows errorErrors (for example a variable store that could not be saved), the full assembled prompt and raw response of every LLM call, and deterministic statement traces. info (default)Everything above plus warnings, one line per LLM call with its effective parameters and duration, think{show=True} reasoning, log() messages. debugEverything above plus tool invocations, caught failures and ac"},{"title":"MCP configuration","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp","level":2,"text":"Pass a JSON file using the VS Code mcp.json shape: a top-level servers object maps server names to connection settings. MCP tools are available only during explicit agent calls. Generation never starts MCP servers, and record/replay rejects agents."},{"title":"Remote HTTP or SSE server","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-http","level":3,"text":"For a URL-based server, explicitly set \"type\": \"http\" (or \"sse\" for an SSE endpoint). Omitting type defaults to stdio, so a config containing only url and headers is rejected. Keep credentials out of the JSON file. Set SOCIALTOAI_AUTHORIZATION in the environment before running SPL. Its value must be the complete header value required by the service (for example Bearer YOUR_TOKEN if that service re"},{"title":"Local stdio server","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-stdio","level":3,"text":"A stdio server runs a local executable that speaks MCP over stdin/stdout. Replace the script path below with your MCP server's entry point. Keep the executable in command and its arguments as separate strings in args, not as one shell command."},{"title":"Configuration fields","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-fields","level":3,"text":"Server names use letters, digits, underscores, dots or hyphens. They are the names used in restrict mcp, not a server's URL or executable name. FieldApplies toMeaning typeAll\"stdio\" (default), \"http\" or \"sse\". Always specify it for remote servers. commandstdio, requiredNon-empty string: executable name or path. argsstdio, optionalArray of argument strings, such as [\"server.js\", \"--port\", \"3000\"]. "},{"title":"Variables, paths and unsupported settings","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-variables","level":3,"text":"${workspaceFolder} expands to SPL's --cwd, not the folder containing the JSON file. ${env:NAME} expands an environment variable in command, args, env, cwd, url or headers values. A missing variable is a configuration error, including during --check. The --mcp filename is relative to the invoking directory, independently of --cwd. The config may be anywhere; .vscode/mcp.json is a convention, not a "},{"title":"Validate, run and restrict access","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-usage","level":3,"text":"With a script and config in the current directory: --check validates script syntax, JSON structure, settings and variable expansion without starting MCP servers or making LLM calls. It does not verify credentials, endpoint reachability, executable availability or tool names. Use server names from the JSON keys to limit tool access: restrict mcp 'socialtoai/tool_name' allows just one tool; replace "},{"title":"Variable store","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#store","level":2,"text":"Stores are independent of generation recordings. --replay rejects both --store and --store-module before loading user modules or writing state. Scripts can keep state between runs. --store vars.json loads top-level variables from the file at start and writes them back at exit - also on failure. Functions are not persisted. The file must contain a JSON object; writes are atomic (temp file + rename)"},{"title":"Custom stores","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#custom-store","level":3,"text":"--store-module ./mystore.mjs plugs in your own persistence. The module exports createStore() (or a default export of that shape) returning an object with load() and save(vars): ⚠️ Synchronous interface load() and save() are synchronous, so a store must complete its I/O before returning. createStore() itself may be async. save() receives plain JSON-compatible values (vars), never functions."},{"title":"Authentication","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#login","level":2,"text":"LLM calls go to GitHub Copilot, so the runtime needs credentials. Run spl login once: it does nothing if the runtime is already authenticated (an earlier login, a Copilot CLI or gh login, or COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN / GH_TOKEN / GITHUB_TOKEN); otherwise it starts GitHub's device flow, prints a one-time code and a URL, waits until you confirm in the browser, verifies the token with the Copilot runtime "},{"title":"Backend","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#backends","level":2,"text":"Each live call opens a fresh session through the GitHub Copilot SDK. Generation (? and think) exposes no tools and aborts unexpected permission requests. Only explicit agent sessions expose runtime and configured MCP tools under active restrictions. Pass context explicitly; previous calls are not implicit conversation history."},{"title":"Offline generation record/replay","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#record-replay","level":2,"text":"Record creates a new JSONL file and never overwrites one. Replay needs no credentials and never constructs a Copilot client or starts MCP servers. Both modes reject agents. Recording captures successful backend responses, including invalid JSON that subsequently triggers a repair; transport errors are not recorded. Requests must match exactly and in order: call kind, assembled prompt (including sc"},{"title":"Reproducibility","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#reproducibility","level":2,"text":"Control flow, arithmetic and interpolation are deterministic. For LLM calls reproducibility is best effort: The Copilot SDK has no temperature or seed setting, so the language has none either. The shared reasoning modifier maps to SDK effort; unsupported effort logs a warning and uses the model default. Use schemas, assertions, bounded generation repair, and offline record/replay to contain varian"},{"title":"VS Code extension","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html","level":1,"text":"Syntax highlighting, snippets and editing support for .spl scripts. The extension lives in the vscode/ folder of the repository."},{"title":"Features","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#features","level":2,"text":"🎨"},{"title":"Install","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#install","level":2,"text":"For development, open the vscode/ folder in VS Code and press F5 to launch an Extension Development Host."},{"title":"Snippets","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#snippets","level":2,"text":"Type the prefix, then Tab. AreaPrefixes Control flowdef if ifelse for while let LLMask askobj askschema think agent model Failurestry retry (bounded schema repair) assert fail success Scopesenforce forbid restrict restrictnet restrictexec restrictmcp restrictmulti Toolsread_file write_file web_fetch web_search shell"},{"title":"Develop & test","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#develop","level":2,"text":"test/*.spl contain scope assertions: a line # ^^^ scope.name asserts the scope of the columns under the carets on the line above (- scope asserts absence). test/run-assertions.mjs runs them with vscode-textmate. test/check-examples.mjs tokenizes every script in ../lang/examples and fails on invalid scopes or on strings/blocks that are left open at the end of a file. 🔄 Keep the grammar in sync Whe"},{"title":"Examples","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html","level":1,"text":"Working scripts from lang/examples/, synchronized with their executable sources. Most run from lang/; test repair runs against the npm project selected by --cwd. That option controls relative I/O and tool directories, not the script filename."},{"title":"Commit message","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#commit-message","level":2,"text":"Read the staged diff with raw shell, restricted to git. Generation itself has no tools. A deterministic validator permits at most three message attempts; it never reruns the shell command. 🔑Pattern: deterministic validator The model proposes; is_valid() decides. The loop gives it three tries, and the final assert makes sure a script never succeeds with an invalid message."},{"title":"Triage issues","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#triage-issues","level":2,"text":"Classify issues against a strict schema with enum labels/priorities and required reason text. Up to two repairs correct format/schema errors, without retrying transport errors. This generation-only example supports offline record/replay."},{"title":"Extract an invoice","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#extract-invoice","level":2,"text":"Validate invoice shape with a nested schema and bounded repair, then independently check arithmetic with assertions. A business-rule failure is not automatically repaired."},{"title":"Code review as a CI gate","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#code-review","level":2,"text":"An explicit agent inspects source under filesystem and git restrictions, then tool-free generation structures its findings. The report write is restricted to ./reports, and high-severity findings fail the CI gate. A request to only review is guidance, not a read-only filesystem capability."},{"title":"Release notes","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#release-notes","level":2,"text":"Generate release notes from recent git history and save them to ./out/RELEASE_NOTES.md. The history-reading phase restricts executable names to git; the separate file-writing phase restricts filesystem access to ./out. Note how rules and restrictions nest."},{"title":"Web digest","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#web-digest","level":2,"text":"Summarize a web page with network access limited to a single host - and prove the sandbox holds by catching a blocked request to a second host."},{"title":"Fix failing tests","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#fix-failing-tests","level":2,"text":"Run raw npm test in the project selected by --cwd, invoke an explicit repair agent after a failure, and repeat up to three rounds. Files are restricted to the project and executable names to npm. Avoiding test edits is guidance, not a filesystem write prohibition. This workflow intentionally performs actions and is not record/replay-compatible; review its changes. 🔑Pattern: failure as input A fai"},{"title":"Search with MCP","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#mcp","level":2,"text":"Configure a trusted MCP server named docs exposing search, then run this explicit agent with --mcp. The exact docs/search selector narrows MCP access. Services are trusted; scopes limit calls, not their internal I/O. Generation-only calls never start MCP servers."},{"title":"Translate docs","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#translate-docs","level":2,"text":"Read the document once, generate German, Polish, and Japanese translations without tools, and write each exact response to a script-chosen path. Run from lang/ so the source document resolves correctly. No model is responsible for choosing or confirming the output path."},{"title":"Journal with persistent state","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#journal","level":2,"text":"A rolling journal that remembers previous runs through the variable store. Top-level variables are saved on exit and reloaded on the next run, so state survives between runs."},{"title":"Feature tours","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#tours","level":2,"text":"Small scripts that exercise one area each."},{"title":"basics.spl - functions, objects and LLM calls","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#basics","level":3,"text":"Compare a computed Fibonacci number with an integer model response, then request a structured joke and a pet story. This tour includes LLM calls and requires authentication."},{"title":"hi.spl - compact end-to-end tour","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#hi","level":3,"text":"Use gpt-5.5 for a structured joke, reasoning and a greeting, then write the deterministic value fib(10) under ./out."},{"title":"Live tours","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#live-tours","level":3,"text":"live.spl - a quick smoke test of ? with len/fmt, think, enforce/forbid and assert against the real model. tools-live.spl - read_file, write_file and think{show=True} inside restrict './sandbox'. Run it after mkdir sandbox; echo hello world > sandbox/note.txt. restrict-live.spl - asks the model to read secret.txt outside the sandbox and shows the runtime blocking it, returning a restriction_violati"},{"title":"Failures & errors","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html","level":1,"text":"Failures stop execution unless caught. Format, validation, transport, tool, and capability failures have distinct categories so recovery does not accidentally repeat external actions."},{"title":"Failure payload","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#payload","level":2,"text":"line is 1-based and command contains the innermost failing statement's source line. Backend lifecycle checks outside a statement may use line zero and an empty command. Caught payloads are ordinary dict values."},{"title":"Status codes","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#status-codes","level":2,"text":"CodeCause invalid_formatMalformed strict JSON, or a value that does not match the selected fmt type. validation_failedJSON parsed, but did not satisfy the schema. Reason includes field paths. runtime_errorInvalid modifiers/schema, undeclared local assignment, undefined name, type/arity/index/arithmetic error. assertion_failedA business-rule assertion was false. explicit_failureThe script executed "},{"title":"try / except","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#try-except","level":2,"text":"The handler's name and variables declared in either branch belong to the same function/script scope. Guidance and restriction contexts unwind before the handler runs. Syntax errors occur before execution and cannot be caught. Runtime failures do not roll back prior writes or variable changes."},{"title":"assert, fail, success","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#assert-fail-success","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Assertions","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#assert","level":3,"text":"assert condition ['message'] checks a business rule independently of schema repair."},{"title":"Explicit failure","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#fail","level":3,"text":"fail reason stops with explicit_failure, unless caught."},{"title":"Successful termination","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#success","level":3,"text":"success [value] immediately ends the whole script, including from a function. return exits the current function, or the script when used at top level."},{"title":"Bounded generation repair","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#retry","level":2,"text":"This permits one initial call plus two corrections. Only invalid JSON and schema mismatches use that budget. Each correction receives the previous answer and validation errors. Prompt/modifier expressions run once, and generation exposes no tools. Transport/model errors are not retried; external assertions are not repaired. Agents reject nonzero repair budgets. Do not retry entire side-effecting b"},{"title":"Uncaught failures and CLI errors","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#uncaught","level":2,"text":"An uncaught script failure prints its payload to stdout and exits with code 1. Syntax/usage errors go to stderr with code 2; internal errors use 70. Invalid recording files are usage errors. Replay mismatch/exhaustion is catchable, while unused entries are checked at backend close and still make the CLI fail before printing a success value. Opt-in variable stores save even after a script failure. "},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html","level":1,"text":"Start with exact raw tools, add schema-validated generation, and opt into agents only when the model should choose actions. Syntax checks, raw tools, and recorded replay need no Copilot login."},{"title":"Requirements","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#requirements","level":2,"text":"Node.js 20 or newer. An authenticated GitHub Copilot: run spl login once (see Authentication). Not needed for --check or for scripts without LLM commands."},{"title":"Install & build","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#install","level":2,"text":"The interpreter lives in the lang/ folder of the repository. npm install also runs prepare, which compiles the TypeScript sources to dist/. The package exposes two binaries, spl and spl-lang, both pointing at dist/cli.js."},{"title":"Your first run","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#first-run","level":2,"text":"Check the syntax. --check parses the script and exits - no LLM, no side effects. Log in to Copilot. Once per machine; it opens GitHub's device flow (a code you confirm in the browser). Run it against Copilot. The Copilot SDK is used with your login. Or use npx. From a checkout, or as npx spl-lang once published. ℹ️ stdout vs. stderr The value passed to success (or to a top-level return) goes to st"},{"title":"Write a script","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#hello","level":2,"text":"Create hello.spl: 💡 Literal braces in prompts Every string interpolates {expr}. To write a literal brace - for example a JSON template - double it ({{ }}), escape it (\\{ \\}) or use a raw string r'{\"a\": 1}'."},{"title":"Exit codes","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#exit-codes","level":2,"text":"CodeMeaning 0The script finished (reached success, a top-level return or the end). 1Uncaught failure - the failure payload is printed to stdout. 2Syntax error or command-line usage error. 70Internal interpreter error."},{"title":"Replay generation offline","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#record-replay","level":2,"text":"The second run needs no model credentials. Requests must match exactly and in order. Replay permits local reads but blocks writes, shell, network, agents, and stores. Recordings contain prompts and raw responses; protect them as sensitive data. See record/replay."},{"title":"Breaking changes in 0.3","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#migration","level":2,"text":"OldNew read_file{path='a.txt'} 'Return it'read_file('a.txt'); interpret the result with a separate generation call. Tools with missing parametersRequired positional arguments, or explicit agent '...' for model-selected actions. instruct / avoidUse enforce / forbid for scoped prompt guidance. think{level='high'}?{reasoning='high'} or think{reasoning='high'}. let x += 1, let d.key = 2x += 1, d.key ="},{"title":"Repository tour","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#repo-tour","level":2,"text":"PathContents LANG-PRD.mdProduct requirements - the language specification. lang/The interpreter: src/, tests/, examples/ and docs/syntax.ebnf. vscode/VS Code extension: TextMate grammar, snippets, language configuration, tests. docs/This documentation site."},{"title":"Run the tests","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#tests","level":2,"text":"For authenticated live checks, explicitly set SPL_LIVE_TESTS=1. In PowerShell: $env:SPL_LIVE_TESTS='1'; npm test, then remove the environment variable. Documentation snippets and example syntax are checked offline."},{"title":"Grammar (EBNF)","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html","level":1,"text":"SPL 0.3 syntax, synchronized from lang/docs/syntax.ebnf. Modifier names are checked while parsing; expression values and schemas are checked at runtime."},{"title":"Notation","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html#notation","level":2,"text":"{ x } means repetition, [ x ] means optional, and | separates alternatives. Statements end at a newline, semicolon, closing block, or end of input. Opening block braces must share the keyword's line. Comments begin with #."},{"title":"Complete grammar","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html#program","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Syntax versus semantics","page":"Grammar (EBNF)","href":"grammar.html#semantics","level":2,"text":"let declares in the current block scope; reads and assignment search enclosing lexical scopes. Blocks and conditionals yield their final expression or None. Raw tools are ordinary function calls. Generation and agents share expression-valued modifiers; log has its own level modifier. See language basics and generation contracts for runtime rules. Run node docs/build-search-index.mjs from the repos"},{"title":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html","level":1,"text":"SPL is a small Python-like language for reliable AI workflows: exact raw tools, schema-validated generation, and explicit agents. Bounded repair and offline replay contain model variance without silently repeating external actions."},{"title":"Why SPL?","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#why","level":2,"text":"Scripts that glue LLMs to files, shells and the web usually end up as a pile of string-building and ad-hoc parsing. SPL makes the interesting parts first-class and keeps the rest boring on purpose. 🧠"},{"title":"Design tenets","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#tenets","level":2,"text":"🎯Best-effort reproducibility Control flow and interpolation are deterministic; live model output is not. Schemas and assertions validate answers, while exact generation record/replay supports offline regression tests. 🛡️Least privilege by restriction Generation has no tools. Explicit agents and raw tools remain allow-by-default; narrow actions with runtime-checked restrict blocks. Shell and MCP "},{"title":"How a run works","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#how-it-works","level":2,"text":"ParseStrict lexer & parser. Unknown modifiers are syntax errors. → InterpretDeterministic evaluation of statements and expressions. → AssembleConstraints, restrictions and modifier hints are prepended to the prompt. → CallCopilot SDK. Tool calls are intercepted and checked. → ResultValue of success to stdout, logs to stderr."},{"title":"Where to next","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#explore","level":2,"text":"🚀"},{"title":"Non-goals","page":"Write programs where prompts are syntax.","href":"index.html#non-goals","level":2,"text":"Bit-for-bit reproducible live model output, a general workflow cache, or transactional external actions. Being a full Python implementation - only a reasonable subset is supported."},{"title":"Architecture","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html","level":1,"text":"A small tree-walking interpreter with explicit boundaries between raw I/O, generation, and agent actions."},{"title":"Execution pipeline","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#pipeline","level":2,"text":"The lexer produces positioned tokens and string interpolation parts. The recursive-descent parser produces a typed AST and rejects invalid structure and unknown/duplicate modifier names. The interpreter evaluates modifier expressions, validates configuration, and runs the selected operation. Raw function call: evaluate arguments, check restrictions, run ToolHost, return exact output. Generation: e"},{"title":"Modules","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#modules","level":2,"text":"SourceResponsibility lexer.ts / parser.ts / ast.tsTokenization, syntax validation, typed statements and expressions. interpreter.tsLexical block scopes, expression-valued blocks/conditionals, leveled logging, control flow, raw built-ins, generation options, repair, scoped guidance/restrictions, source locations. values.ts / builtins.tsValue model, conversions, ordinary functions and collection met"},{"title":"Execution model","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#execution-model","level":2,"text":"Each block creates a child environment; function calls link to their lexical closure rather than the caller. Reads and assignments search outward for the first declaration, while let declares/redeclares locally and shadows enclosing names. Each loop iteration and exception handler has its own bindings. Child declarations do not leak into parents or persistent globals. Block/conditional expressions"},{"title":"Generation and repair lifecycle","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#llm-call","level":2,"text":"Evaluate modifiers once and reject invalid values/combinations. Compile the schema before invoking the backend. Evaluate the prompt once; assemble guidance, restriction context, hints, and schema. Call a fresh generation session with no available tools or MCP servers. Parse strict JSON and validate the schema. Only format/schema failures may use the bounded repair budget. Agents use the same final"},{"title":"Embedding","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#embedding","level":2,"text":"run returns success with value and hasValue, or failure with its payload. Options include backend, logger, cwd, optional store, model override, and maximum call depth. Other exports include authentication helpers, variable stores, MCP configuration helpers, and related TypeScript types."},{"title":"Recording and replay","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#recording","level":2,"text":"Wrap a live backend with new RecordingBackend(backend, file), or use new ReplayBackend(file) without constructing a live backend. Close either backend after use; replay checks for unused entries on close. Do not publish a successful result until that check completes. Records contain raw backend responses, not just parsed values, so replay re-exercises parsing, schema checks, repair prompts, and de"},{"title":"Extension points","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#extending","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"Custom backend","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#backend-interface","level":3,"text":"Implement LlmBackend.call(req) returning text, optional reasoning, and effective-parameter metadata, plus close(). The request carries the operation kind, assembled prompt, requested model/effort, reasoning display flag, restriction snapshot, and ToolHost. Backend exceptions become script-level llm_error; runtime Failure values retain their category."},{"title":"Custom store","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#variable-store","level":3,"text":"Implement synchronous load() and save(vars), exported through a module factory. Stores are independent of model recordings and are forbidden in replay mode."},{"title":"Validation","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#tests","level":3,"text":"The default suite is offline, using scripted backends and temporary files. Set SPL_LIVE_TESTS=1 explicitly to enable authenticated Copilot checks. Editor scope tests and documentation snippet tests cover the public language surfaces."},{"title":"Language basics","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html","level":1,"text":"The deterministic core of SPL: Python-flavoured expressions, braces, lexical block scopes, and explicit assignment. Model calls are distinct expressions, not implicit behavior of ordinary functions."},{"title":"Program structure","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#structure","level":2,"text":"A script is an optional @{…} directive header followed by statements. # starts a comment that runs to the end of the line."},{"title":"Directives","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#directives","level":3,"text":"The header directive is model, whose value must be a plain string without interpolation. Precedence is CLI --model, per-call model, header, then SDK default."},{"title":"Variables","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#variables","level":2,"text":"let name = expression declares or redeclares a local. Assignment and compound operators (+= -= *= /= //= %=) update existing locals; they do not use let. Member/item targets also omit let. Unknown reads and assignment to an undeclared local are catchable runtime errors."},{"title":"Scope","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#scope","level":3,"text":"let name = … binds in the current scope. Every block creates a lexical child scope. Inner declarations shadow enclosing names and cannot be accessed from a parent scope. Reads and assignments search outward through the full lexical chain for the first declaration. There is no hoisting: a local appears when execution binds it. Conditionals, loops, exception handlers, and guidance/restriction blocks"},{"title":"Block and conditional expressions","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#block-expressions","level":3,"text":"A block or selected if/elif/else branch yields its final expression. An empty branch, a block ending in a statement, or an unmatched conditional returns None. Only the selected branch runs. {} remains an empty dictionary; use { pass } for a block yielding None. Functions still require explicit return."},{"title":"Persistence","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#persistence","level":3,"text":"Top-level variables can be saved between runs with opt-in --store, even after a script failure. Persistence is not a transaction or an action log and is forbidden during replay. See CLI & runtime."},{"title":"Types & values","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#types","level":2,"text":"type()LiteralsNotes 'None'NoneFalsy. 'bool'True FalsePython spelling. 'number'42 3.14 1e-3One numeric type. int() truncates, float() parses. 'str''a' \"a\" '''a''' r'a'Indexed by Unicode character. 'list'[1, 2, 3]Mutable, trailing comma allowed. 'dict'{'a': 1}Keys must be strings. Insertion ordered. Also readable as d.a. 'function'def f(x) { … }First-class values. Model calls can request typed outpu"},{"title":"Truthiness","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#truthiness","level":3,"text":"None, False, 0, empty strings, empty lists and empty dicts are falsy. Everything else (including functions) is truthy."},{"title":"Strings & interpolation","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#strings","level":2,"text":"All four quote styles interpolate {expression}. Any expression is allowed, including calls and indexing. Substitution is as is: strings raw, True/False/None in Python spelling, lists and dicts as JSON."},{"title":"Quote styles & escapes","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#quotes","level":3,"text":"FormBehaviour '…' \"…\"Single line; escapes and interpolation. '''…''' \"\"\"…\"\"\"May span lines; escapes and interpolation. Ideal for prompts. r'…'Raw: no escapes, no interpolation. Valid escapes: \\n \\t \\r \\0 \\\\ \\' \\\" \\{ \\}. Anything else is a syntax error, and so is a stray single } in a string - write }} or \\}."},{"title":"Operators","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#operators","level":2,"text":"Lowest to highest precedence: OperatorsDescription x if cond else yConditional expression. orLogical or (short-circuit). andLogical and (short-circuit). notLogical negation. == != < <= > >= in not inComparison and membership. == is deep for lists and dicts. Ordering works on numbers, strings and lists. + -Add / subtract. + also joins two strings or two lists - mixing types is an error (use interpo"},{"title":"Control flow","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#control-flow","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"if / elif / else","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#if","level":3,"text":""},{"title":"for & while","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#loops","level":3,"text":"for iterates lists, strings (characters) and dicts (keys). Several names unpack list items, which pairs well with items() and enumerate(). break, continue and pass work as in Python."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#functions","level":2,"text":"Define with def. Functions take positional arguments only (the count is checked), return None unless return is used, can recurse (up to a maximum call depth of 200) and close over their defining scope. return leaves the innermost function immediately, from inside any nested block, loop, try or constraint block; at the top level of a script it ends the script like success, optionally with a value. "},{"title":"Built-ins","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#builtins-brief","level":2,"text":"A compact standard library is available without imports: len, range, sorted, json_parse, log, and Python-style methods on strings, lists and dicts. The full list is on the Built-ins page."},{"title":"Generation, agents & tools","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html","level":1,"text":"Exact I/O, model generation, and model-directed actions are different operations. SPL makes the boundary explicit so validation and retries cannot silently repeat external actions."},{"title":"Generation: ?","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html#ask","level":2,"text":"? generates a value without runtime tools, built-in Copilot tools, or MCP access. It is a prefix operator: ?'a' + b means (?'a') + b. Pass external information explicitly: Each call starts a fresh session; previous answers are not implicit conversation history. Generated text is trimmed; raw tool output is not."},{"title":"Composable modifiers","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html#modifiers","level":2,"text":"?, think, and agent share these modifiers. Braces are optional. Values are expressions evaluated once; names must be known and unique. Invalid names are syntax errors; invalid values are catchable runtime errors before the model call. ModifierValuesMeaning len'word', 'sentence', 'paragraph', 'long'Prompt hint, not a hard length limit. fmt'integer', 'float', 'array', 'object', 'text', 'boolean', 'j"},{"title":"Validated structured output","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html#schema","level":2,"text":"Schemas use synchronous JSON Schema draft-07 with Ajv strict mode. Types, enums, nested properties, required fields, arrays, numeric/string limits, and standard synchronous composition are supported. Unknown keywords, invalid schemas, unresolved remote references, and asynchronous schemas fail before generation. There is no coercion, default insertion, or removal of extra fields. Custom formats/pl"},{"title":"Output formats","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html#fmt-object","level":3,"text":"FormatRequired responseSPL value 'integer'A JSON number with no fractional part, such as 55.number 'float'Any finite JSON number, such as 3.14 or 42.number 'array'A JSON array, such as [1, 2, 3].list 'object'A JSON object, such as {\"ok\": true}. Arrays are rejected.dict 'text'Plain text, trimmed without JSON parsing. This is the default.str 'boolean'JSON true or false, not quoted strings or Python "},{"title":"Bounded repair, not action retry","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html#repairs","level":3,"text":"repairs=2 permits at most three model calls: the initial call plus two corrections. Only invalid_format and validation_failed trigger correction. The next prompt contains the prior answer and validation errors. Prompt/modifier expressions are not reevaluated. Authentication, timeout, transport, tool, and restriction errors are not repaired; assertions outside the call are not repaired either. Gene"},{"title":"Think shorthand","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html#think","level":2,"text":"think is generation-only shorthand with default medium reasoning. It accepts exactly the same modifiers as ?, so reasoning and structured output no longer require two separate calls."},{"title":"Explicit agents","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html#agent","level":2,"text":"agent enables model-directed runtime tools and configured MCP tools. It defaults to medium reasoning. Restrict its capabilities deliberately; raw tools and agents are allow-by-default outside restrictions. Only agents expose tools to Copilot. Any permission request during generation is rejected and aborts the call. Agent tools still use the runtime restriction checks; unconfigured MCP servers and "},{"title":"Scoped guidance: enforce & forbid","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html#constraints","level":2,"text":"enforce adds positive guidance and forbid adds negative guidance for every model call inside the block. These are the language's scoped prompt-guidance keywords. Guidance applies dynamically to calls made inside the block, including called functions, and unwinds afterward. Each block has a child variable scope, so declare answer outside and assign inside when it must remain visible. Guidance is no"},{"title":"Exact raw tools","page":"Generation, agents & tools","href":"llm-commands.html#tools","level":2,"text":"FunctionResultRestriction read_file(path)Exact UTF-8 contentsfs write_file(path, content)Write confirmation textfs web_fetch(url)HTTP response body textnet web_search(query)Search result textnet shell(command)Combined stdout/stderr; nonzero exit raises tool_errorexec, plus inspected fs/net arguments Arguments are positional, required strings. These functions never invoke a model, never ask it to f"},{"title":"Restrictions","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html","level":1,"text":"restrict narrows raw tools and explicit agent actions. Outside restrictions they are allow-by-default. Generation has no tools at all, and replay further restricts execution to local reads and recorded generation."},{"title":"The four forms","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#forms","level":2,"text":"FormEffect restrict './target/out' { … }Reads and writes are allowed only inside the folder. Same as restrict fs. restrict net 'api.example.com' { … }Network access is allowed only to the listed hosts. restrict exec 'npm,git' { … }Shell commands are allowed only for the listed executables. restrict mcp 'docs/search,issues' { … }Only the named MCP tools or servers may be called. Servers must be con"},{"title":"Two layers of enforcement","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#enforcement","level":2,"text":"🗣️1 · GuidanceThe runtime tells the model about the active restrictions in the prompt, so it plans within them. 🚧2 · Hard enforcementThe runtime intercepts every file, network and exec call and blocks any that violate the active scopes. A blocked call raises a restriction_violation failure. It is catchable with try/except:"},{"title":"Nesting","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#nesting","level":2,"text":"Restrictions are scoped to their block and can be nested. Nested restrictions of the same kind intersect: they can only narrow the allowed set, never widen it. Different kinds combine freely."},{"title":"Matching rules","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#rules","level":2,"text":""},{"title":"File system","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#fs","level":3,"text":"Paths are canonicalized before checking: made absolute relative to --cwd, .. removed, symbolic links resolved - including dangling links, whose targets are followed so a write cannot escape through them. A path is allowed when it is the allowed folder itself or inside it. On Windows the comparison is case-insensitive."},{"title":"Network","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#net","level":3,"text":"net matches exact hostnames and *.example.com wildcards. Entries may be bare hosts or URLs (the host is extracted; ports are ignored). Only http: and https: URLs are fetched. Redirects are followed manually (at most 5) and the host is re-checked on every hop. web_search is network access too: it queries html.duckduckgo.com, so a restrict net scope must list that host for searches to work."},{"title":"Executables","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#exec","level":3,"text":"exec matches the executable name. On Windows .exe, .cmd, .bat and .com suffixes are ignored. Inside a restrict exec block, commands must be named without a path (git, not /usr/bin/git). Shell commands in pipelines and chains are checked against active restrictions: executables are checked against restrict exec, and path-like/URL arguments and redirection targets are checked against active fs and n"},{"title":"MCP servers and tools","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#mcp","level":3,"text":"Load servers with --mcp. A selector such as docs or docs/* allows every configured tool from that server; docs/search allows only its search tool. Names are case-sensitive. Nested scopes intersect, and cannot widen the configuration's optional tools allowlist. Disallowed servers and tools are removed before creating each LLM session. Every MCP permission request is checked again before approval; a"},{"title":"What is covered","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#what-is-guarded","level":2,"text":"CallerChecked? Raw function calls (read_file(...), shell(...))yes Tool calls during explicit agent operationsyes Generation (? / think)No tools exposed; permission attempts abort the call. MCP tools from --mcpconfiguration and MCP scope checked Any other Copilot permission requestrejected Plain SPL code (arithmetic, log, variables)nothing to restrict 🔮 Future direction Raw tools and agents remain"}];
